  </div>
<section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">Xuanzang: The Monk Behind the Pagoda</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">The pagoda was built to honor <strong class="text-stone-800">Xuanzang</strong> (602-664 AD), one of the most important figures in Buddhist history. In 629 AD, defying an imperial travel ban, he set out alone from Chang'an (Xi'an) on a 17-year, 25,000-kilometer journey to India to collect original Buddhist scriptures. He crossed the Gobi Desert, the Tian Shan mountains, and survived bandit attacks, snowstorms, and near-starvation.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">When he returned in 645 AD, he brought back 657 Sanskrit texts, 150 relics, and 7 statues. Emperor Taizong built the Big Wild Goose Pagoda to house the scriptures and a translation academy where Xuanzang spent the rest of his life translating 74 texts into Chinese. His journey became the inspiration for the classic novel <em>Journey to the West</em> — the Monkey King story that every Chinese person knows. A statue of Xuanzang stands in the square south of the pagoda.</p>
    </section>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>